Transactional databases store vast amounts of unstructured text — clinical notes, customer reviews, support tickets, invoices, and property descriptions. Historically, working with this text meant building complex application code: You had to export data from the database, call external model endpoints, write custom prompt wrappers, parse unpredictable responses, manage retry logic, and update database records. This application-side loop creates operational friction. It introduces query latency, bloats application code, increases model token costs, and creates data compliance risks by moving sensitive information across multiple network boundaries. We built AlloyDB AI to bring Gemini world knowledge directly into your operational database. Rather than treating artificial intelligence as a disconnected external service, we integrated model intelligence directly into the database engine. Today, we are announcing the preview of two new SQL functions in the AlloyDB AI query engine — ai.classify and ai.extract — available in the google_ml_integration extension (version 1.6.1 or later) on PostgreSQL 18, allowing you to perform classification and structured extraction directly inside your database using standard SQL. Supported by smart batching for AI functions (previously known as AI Function Acceleration), these functions deliver production scalability and performance. Now, let’s take a look at these new functions and how to use them. Classifying text directly in SQL with ai.classify The ai.classify function categorizes text into user-defined classes without requiring manual prompt engineering. You can specify categories as a simple list of labels or provide a list containing label names and descriptions to guide model context. Key capabilities include: Single-label classification ( output_mode => 'single' ) to assign the single best category Multi-label classification ( output_mode => 'multi' ) to return all matching categories Category descriptions using 2D arrays to provide explicit context for each label Few-shot prompting by supplying inline examples Flexible execution modes for individual rows ( TEXT ), arrays ( TEXT[] ), and cursors ( REFCURSOR ) Here’s a quick example showing how you can classify customer feedback directly within a SQL query: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', "SET google_ml_integration.enable_preview_ai_functions = TRUE;

CREATE TABLE product_reviews (id INT, review TEXT);

INSERT INTO product_reviews VALUES
(1, 'Fantastic, would buy again!'),
(2, 'Broken on arrival.'),
(3, 'Mediocre, not worth the price.');

SELECT unnest(
 ai.classify(
 inputs => ARRAY(SELECT review FROM product_reviews ORDER BY id),
 categories => ARRAY[
 ['positive', 'Good sentiment'],
 ['neutral', 'Neither good nor bad sentiment'],
 ['negative', 'Bad sentiment']
 ],
 batch_size => 5
 )
) AS sentiment;

-- Expected Output:
-- sentiment
-- ---------
-- positive
-- negative
-- negative"), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7f2f32f4a650>)])]> Extracting structured JSON with ai.extract The ai.extract function transforms unstructured text into structured JSON data matching your target schema — specified as a JSON schema or a text list of fields. It handles system instructions internally, saving you from writing prompt templates or response parsers. Key capabilities include: Schema flexibility supporting standard JSON schemas or plain text field lists Automatic transformation of unstructured text into machine-readable JSON Overloaded function signatures for single rows, arrays, and cursor batching Here’s an example extracting clinical fields from free-form medical notes: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'SET google_ml_integration.enable_preview_ai_functions = TRUE;

SELECT ai.extract( 
 input => 'Patient Jane Smith, 52 years old, visited the clinic on Oct 25. She is currently experiencing a mild fever, persistent cough, and severe body aches. The doctor prescribed Ibuprofen and Amoxicillin.',
 output_schema => '{
 "patient_name": {"type": "string"},
 "age": {"type": "integer"},
 "symptoms": {
 "type": "array",
 "items": {"type": "string"}
 },
 "prescribed_medications": {
 "type": "array",
 "items": {"type": "string"}
 }
 }'
);

--- Expected output 
-- {
-- "patient_name": "Jane Smith",
-- "age": 52,
-- "symptoms": [
-- "mild fever",
-- "persistent cough",
-- "severe body aches"
-- ],
-- "prescribed_medications": [
-- "Ibuprofen",
-- "Amoxicillin"
-- ]
-- }'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7f2f33074c90>)])]> Speeding up queries with smart batching Running AI evaluations row-by-row can slow down query execution. AlloyDB addresses this with smart batching for AI functions. When you use ai.classify or ai.extract in SQL WHERE clause expressions, the database engine automatically groups inputs into optimized micro-batches before sending them to Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform models. Smart batching delivers significant performance and efficiency improvements: Faster inference for query execution compared to row-at-a-time evaluations Reduces total model token spend by optimizing input prompt overhead Real-world industry applications Native classification and extraction functions support operational workflows across key industry sectors: Healthcare: Securely convert free-text clinical notes into structured records containing patient names, symptoms, and prescribed medications, keeping patient data safely within the confines of the database. Financial services: Extract vendor details, invoice dates, and line-item totals from scanned document text. Legal technology: Pull governing law, liability caps, and termination notice periods from contract documents. Real estate: Extract property types, bedroom counts, and price ranges from descriptive listing text. Knowledge graphs: Extract entities and relationships from documents to populate PostgreSQL Property Graph (PGQ) structures. Next steps and getting started We are continuing to expand in-database AI capabilities in AlloyDB, with upcoming enhancements planned for advanced cost optimization, line-level provenance tracking, and query pushdowns beyond WHERE clauses. To start using ai.classify and ai.extract in your database: Review the AlloyDB AI Query Engine documentation . Explore the AlloyDB release notes .

As many developers use agentic tools to create more code faster than ever before, the code has gotten more complex and they’re taking longer to find and fix software flaws. Helping to address that pressure is a key concern at Google. We know that software development velocity is increasing, as much as 2x for some teams , and the average developer fix time for software flaws expanded from approximately 59 days a decade ago to more than 170 days in 2025, according to the Mandiant M-Trends 2026 Report . CodeMender: AI powered vulnerability fixes right in GitHub PRs To help you secure more code, more quickly, we’ve built CodeMender, our autonomous code-security agent that can find and fix software flaws at machine speed. We also know that developer tools only succeed if they fit into existing routines. That’s why CodeMender, part of our AI Threat Defense platform , brings automated code fixes directly into the developer workflow. Today, we’re sharing this reference architecture that wires automated CodeMender remediation straight into Git pull requests via Google Cloud Run. What the developer sees Everything happens in the timeline. The scan posts one comment, a table of findings, each finding with an ID, and the two things you can type next. Figure 1: A pull request triggers the scan on Cloud Run. Findings, exploit traces, and proposed patches all come back to the same timeline. Reply /verify 612424da and the exploit attempt comes back in the thread, folded into a collapsible View Exploit Steps & Response Trace block so it doesn't bury the conversation. Reply /fix 612424da and the patch arrives the same way, behind Preview Proposed Code Diff , with a draft pull request already open /approve there. Both commands take all in place of an ID. Figure 2: The initial scan comment provides a table of findings and the actionable slash commands, keeping the developer entirely within the pull request timeline. Nothing asks the developer to leave the pull request or learn a console. Ship evidence, not just a severity CodeMender cm find runs return a confidence score on the vulnerability finding during the code analysis. If the confidence score is below 100%, you can use cm verify to have CodeMender build and simulate an exploit in a sandbox to determine if the vulnerability is actually exploitable. The simulation results can change the severity. Because the verification sandbox is ephemeral, the pipeline automatically streams these artifacts to Google Cloud Storage to preserve an audit trail for compliance and security operations reviews. Figure 3: The /verify command returns a trace of the sandboxed exploit attempt, giving reviewers actionable evidence rather than abstract alerts. Engineer for failure modes in your verification pipeline. If your harness only searches for a successful exploit string, then any test failure — such as a crashed mock server or an execution timeout — can cause the pipeline to fail open and mistakenly label a real vulnerability as safe. Ensure your parser separates true negative results from test crashes. If an exploit fails to complete cleanly, flag the status as inconclusive rather than giving it a clean bill of health. Propose the patch, preview the diff When an exploit proves a finding is real, the next step isn't a ticket, it's a patch: cm fix a1b2c3d4 instructs CodeMender to author one. Figure 4: The /fix command returns a unified code diff, giving reviewers meaningful context to evaluate the patch. If the diff looks good, applying it takes one step. The developer replies /approve . The agent commits the patch to the branch, pushes it, and sets a commit status security/codemender-gate to green. Branch protection is what turns that status into a merge block; without it, the gate is advisory. No stored keys between GitHub and Cloud Run None of this happens until GitHub and Google Cloud trust each other. This agent needs write access to your source code, precisely the credential you don't want sitting in a secret store waiting to leak. GitHub Actions can mint a short-lived OIDC token instead, and Google can be configured to accept it, so nothing long-lived exists to steal. Nearly all the security here rests on one line: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'resource "google_iam_workload_identity_pool_provider" "github" {
 workload_identity_pool_provider_id = "github-provider"

 oidc {
 issuer_uri = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
 }

 attribute_mapping = {
 "google.subject" = "assertion.sub"
 "attribute.repository_id" = "assertion.repository_id"
 }

 # Without this, any repository on GitHub can exchange a token against your pool
 attribute_condition = "assertion.repository_id == '123456789'"
}'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7f2f328a3ad0>)])]> Every GitHub Actions workflow shares that issuer URL, so validating the issuer alone buys you nothing. The condition is the part doing the work. Note what it matches on. assertion.repository would also work here, but it matches a name and names get renamed, or claimed by someone else once you release them. repository_id and repository_owner_id are immutable, which is why Google recommends mapping those instead. Give the exploit somewhere safe to run Verification runs attack code that nobody reviewed, written on demand, pointed at a mock target, doing what the vulnerability class implies. As recent headlines have reminded everyone, the last thing you want is an autonomous agent deciding the fastest way to complete a task is to break out of its container and port-scan your internal network. Before you can trust the verdict, you have to answer two important questions: Where does that code execute, and what can it reach while it does? The answer is to run it as a Cloud Run job. Every Cloud Run instance is sandboxed, and jobs always run in the second-generation execution environment, a microVM with full Linux compatibility. The execution starts, does one thing, and exits. Figure 5: The exploit runs in an instance that holds nothing and lives for one finding. Only the verdict leaves. Then empty the room. Give the job a service account of its own and grant it nothing beyond the API that writes the exploit. It needs no registry access, because Cloud Run imports the container image when you deploy rather than pulling it at the instance start. Skip the Compute Engine default account, which may carry roles/editor depending on your organization policy. Take the network away too: Direct VPC egress into a subnet with no route out means code that can't call anywhere can't exfiltrate what it finds or fetch a second stage. Run one job per finding. The execution ends, the instance goes with it, and the verdict is the only thing that outlives either. Cloud Run sandboxes or agent sandboxes close the last gap. In the instance, process execution is isolated from the parent workload, its environment variables, its secrets, and the Google Cloud metadata server, so the credential the platform hands out by default is out of reach as well. It's built for agents running code they just wrote. Get started today You can build an autonomous GitOps security agent today. Start with the reference implementation at codemender-gitops .
